{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p5-ed963e4d","text":"Error responses are `{ "error": "<message>" }`. Now write the frontend.

Add `apiErrorMessage` helper to useAuth.ts (auto-imported). Actually let me add it in useAuth.ts as an export.

```ts
/**
 * Pulls the message out of an API error. `$fetch` wraps the body in
 * `error.data` for non-2xx responses; anything else falls back to the
 * default message.
 */
export function apiErrorMessage(error: unknown, fallback: string): string {
  if (typeof error === 'object' && error !== null && 'data' in error) {
    const data = (error as { data?: unknown }).data
    if (typeof data === 'object' && data !== null && 'error' in data) {
      const message = (data as { error?: unknown }).error
      if (typeof message === 'string' && message) return message
    }
  }
  if (error instanceof Error && error.message && !error.message.startsWith('FetchError')) {
    return error.message
  }
  return fallback
}
```

Hmm, for network failures $fetch throws FetchError with message "fetch failed" — the fallback is better. Good.

Now write it into useAuth.ts.

Then signin.vue and signup.vue.

Design: use `UPageSection` with a centered narrow card, consistent with rest of the app.

```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
useSeoMeta({ title: 'Sign in — Payvand' })

const route = useRoute()
const router = useRouter()
const { signin, ensureSession } = useAuth()

const email = ref('')
const password = ref('')
const failure = ref('')
const busy = ref(false)

const nextPath = computed(() => safeNextPath(route.query.next))

onMounted(async () => {
  if (await ensureSession()) {
    await router.replace(nextPath.value)
  }
})

async function submit() {
  failure.value = ''
  if (!email.value.trim()) {
    failure.value = 'Enter your email address.'
    return
  }
  if (!password.value) {
    failure.value = 'Enter your password.'
    return
  }

  busy.value = true
  try {
    await signin(email.value, password.value)
    await router.replace(nextPath.value)
  } catch (error) {
    failure.value = apiErrorMessage(error, 'Could not sign in. Check your email and password.')
  } finally {
    busy.value = false
  }
}
</script>
```

Template:
```vue
<template>
  <UPageSection
    title="Sign in"
    description="Your account keeps your payment activity separate from anyone else's."
  >
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-md">
      <UCard>
        <form class="flex flex-col gap-4" @submit.prevent="submit">
          <UFormField label="Email">
            <UInput v-model="email" type="email" autocomplete="email" class="w-full" placeholder="you@example.com" />
          </UFormField>

          <UFormField label="Password">
            <UInput v-model="password" type="password" autocomplete="current-password" class="w-full" placeholder="••••••••" />
          </UFormField>

          <UAlert v-if="failure" color="error" variant="subtle" icon="i-lucide-circle-alert" title="Could not sign in" :description="failure" />

          <UButton type="submit" label="Sign in" icon="i-lucide-log-in" :loading="busy" block />

          <p class="text-center text-sm text-muted">
            No account yet?
            <NuxtLink :to="{ path: '/signup', query: { next: nextPath } }" class="text-primary font-medium">
              Create one
            </NuxtLink>
          </p>
        </form>
      </UCard>
    </div>
  </UPageSection>
</template>
```

Note: `UButton` inside a form — type defaults to "button" in Nuxt UI? Nuxt UI's UButton has a `type` prop that defaults to `button`. So I must set `type="submit"`. Yes I do.

Careful: NuxtLink with `query: { next: nextPath }` — nextPath computed returns string. Fine.

signup.vue similar with confirm password and min length check.

