```

`truncated` prop on UButton exists in Nuxt UI. Let me be careful — `UButton` has `truncated` prop in Nuxt UI v3/v4. Yes it does.

Hmm, to reduce risk, use a span with truncate instead:
```vue
<UButton color="neutral" variant="ghost" :aria-label="user.email" icon="i-lucide-user-round" @click="onSignOut" />
```
That's simpler: an icon button that signs out with aria-label. But then the user's email isn't visible. A small badge showing email is nice. Let's use:

```vue
<span v-if="user" class="hidden md:inline-flex max-w-40 items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full bg-elevated/50 px-2.5 py-1 text-xs text-muted">
  <UIcon name="i-lucide-user-round" class="size-3.5 shrink-0" />
  <span class="truncate">{{ user.email }}</span>
</span>
```

That avoids UButton prop uncertainty. Good.

Also USeparator orientation vertical — in Nuxt UI v4 USeparator exists with `orientation` prop. To be safe, skip the separator.

Now sell.vue and pay.vue: add `definePageMeta({ middleware: 'auth' })`.

And usePaymentApi: send authorization header.

```ts
export function usePaymentApi() {
  const config = useRuntimeConfig()
  const base = config.public.apiBase
  const { token } = useAuth()

  function authHeaders(): Record<string, string> {
    return token.value ? { authorization: `Bearer ${token.value}` } : {}
  }

  async function createPayment(body) {
    return await $fetch(`${base}/api/payments`, {
      method: 'POST',
      body,
      headers: authHeaders()
    })
  }
  ...
}
```

Good.

